What you’re describing makes sense as a context-cue problem, not a lack-of-skill problem. You already have regulation tools, but some of them are bundled with an entire operational state: urgency, vigilance, aggression, suppression of discomfort, mission focus. Around kids, household tasks, or low-stakes frustration, you often need almost the opposite nervous-system configuration.

So I’d deliberately build a second toolkit: civilian regulation. Same underlying control, different cues and outputs.

The yoga side that fits this goal best is restorative yoga, slow Hatha, trauma-informed yoga, and Yoga Nidra. I’d put power yoga lower on the list because it can easily become another arena for effort and performance. The point here is learning to remain present while the body is not mobilizing for action. Long supported poses, slow transitions, relaxed breathing, and noticing sensations without immediately changing them are exactly the reps you want.

For breathing, I would actually avoid relying too heavily on techniques that are strongly tied to your military training. Instead of box breathing, try 4 seconds in / 6–8 seconds out, through the nose, for 3–5 minutes. No holds, no counting cadence that feels tactical. Longer exhalation tends to bias the system toward settling rather than mobilization. You could even pair it with something visually different—lying on the floor, lights dim, hand on your abdomen—so your brain learns, this means home, not mission.

There’s another technique I think is unusually well suited to what you described. Since you can solve someone else’s problem easily, temporarily make your own problem belong to somebody else.

Instead of:

“Why can’t I get this stupid thing done?”

Use:

“If my friend told me he was overwhelmed by this exact thing, what would I tell him to do in the next five minutes?”

That is called psychological distancing or self-distancing. You’re changing perspective without pretending the problem isn’t yours. You already seem naturally good at operating from that perspective.

For the little things, I’d also stop asking yourself to care more. Small tasks often don’t generate enough salience to recruit the same machinery that handles emergencies. Artificially shrink the decision instead:

Don’t: “Clean the kitchen.”
Do: “Put one plate in the dishwasher.”

Then one more.

Your target isn’t motivation. It’s initiation.

A useful sequence when you feel yourself getting wound up is:

Notice → body → name → shrink → act.

Notice: I’m getting activated.

Body: soften your eyes, drop your shoulders, unclench your jaw, slow your hands, exhale longer than you inhale.

Name: irritated, overloaded, impatient, embarrassed, whatever is actually there. You don’t need an essay.

Shrink: What’s the smallest useful action available?

Act: do only that.

That matters around children because children react enormously to tempo. Slower hands, slower feet, softer eyes, quieter voice. You can regulate the room through your own motor behavior before you’ve even fully regulated the feeling.

I’d also experiment with a deliberately physical “home mode” cue. For example, whenever you’re approaching overload around the family:

open hands → soften face → long exhale → lower voice → kneel/sit rather than stand.

Those are almost anti-combat body cues. Over time they can become their own conditioned state. Instead of trying to remove your SEAL conditioning, you’re building another well-trained response beside it.

And patience itself can be trained. One very simple practice is to intentionally encounter tiny frustrations without fixing them immediately: wait through the microwave countdown, let someone finish speaking when you already know the answer, sit at a red light without reaching for your phone, spend two minutes letting a kid struggle with something before helping. You’re practicing tolerating unresolved tension without converting it into action.

That may be one of the missing capacities. In operational environments, unresolved tension often means do something. Parenting and ordinary life contain thousands of moments where the correct response is remain present and don’t escalate.

So your new training split could have four categories:

* Mobilization: strength, hard conditioning, operator skills.
* Movement: Hatha, mobility, controlled end-range work.
* Down-regulation: restorative yoga, Yoga Nidra, long-exhale breathing.
* Emotional control: self-distancing, naming emotions, tolerating frustration, slowing motor behavior.

You don’t need to become less capable of switching on. You’re training a cleaner off switch and dimmer switch, because maximum output isn’t the correct solution to every load.

And if anger ever gets to the point where you’re worried you might lose control around the kids, the immediate move is simple: get them somewhere safe, create physical distance, and come back after your body has come down. If that pattern becomes frequent, working with a clinician who understands high-performance/military backgrounds can be especially useful—not to reteach you discipline, but to help separate the useful skills from the old state they’re attached to.